    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so it's basically just about making sure every non-key column only cares about the primary key. i see how that stops the data from getting messy. are there any specific pitfalls or things i should watch out for when i'm doing this restructuring? like, what usually causes these dependencies to sneak in there in the first place?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A common pitfall is forgetting to properly establish the foreign key relationships between your newly split tables. If you don't link them correctly, you'll end up with data islands, which is as bad as having a single messy table. These dependencies often sneak in because designers try to cram *everything* into one table, thinking it's the easiest way to start, which is a classic trap. It's always better to start simple, even if it means having more tables initially, because the structure of a good database is more robust than the convenience of a single, bloated table.",
      "source": "target",
